                "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                "word": "mudlark",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "ccddec7e-4f6a-4cad-be06-91f591352884",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "mudlarks"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2df38d7f-e17b-39e3-a0a2-177f7b58df3d",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A pig; pork."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic",
                            "slang"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e9b9374a-5d40-3ad1-b116-a500696d3217",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "glosses": [
                            "One who scavenges in river or harbor mud for items of value, especially in London."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic",
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "948b45b3-4877-4784-88e3-88313ac794d4",
                                "sense_id": "e9b9374a-5d40-3ad1-b116-a500696d3217",
                                "text": "Besides these, there were the mudlark and the scupple hunter: the former prowling about at low water, receiving in his small bag such petty packages as he could get from his dishonest friends on board; and the later sneaking about the wharves and quays, under the pretense of wanting work, to pick up everything and anything that came to hand."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f28122a0-46d4-4848-8b35-5b56f46d7884",
                                "sense_id": "e9b9374a-5d40-3ad1-b116-a500696d3217",
                                "text": "She removes only what the water ‘delivers’ to her – items she can see lying on the surface. Unlike some other mudlarks, she does not dig for finds."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ea321c63-7fc5-4a25-9e04-dbca901c9a7e",
                                "sense_id": "e9b9374a-5d40-3ad1-b116-a500696d3217",
                                "text": "Dr. Fiona Haughey, a London archaeologist who has worked on the Thames since the 1990s, said that although some mudlarks are looking for valuables, others are looking for a connection with the everyday objects of a bygone Britain."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "70693bf3-bef7-3ffe-aa26-16d90e457a59",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A child who plays in the mud; a child that spends most of its time in the streets, a street urchin."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "da402560-6f7f-4e8b-a9e5-519a4531822a",
                                "sense_id": "70693bf3-bef7-3ffe-aa26-16d90e457a59",
                                "text": "the children were nothing like inert: a large population of junior mudlarks, so long unwashed that you could hardly make them out, climbed among the ruins, cheerfully playing the games that all children play – pushing wheels with sticks, flipping rusty lids and bottle caps in makeshift tiddlywinks."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bcbd7639-f8fc-3906-8136-40297f3528be",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A soldier of the Royal Engineers."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "slang"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1118fdb9-0a8d-3568-b8fa-5ec6030e5700",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any of various birds that are found in muddy places or build their nests with mud, especially Anthus petrosus and Alauda arvensis."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "regional"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cc07a323-c558-3e2e-a90e-136f434f6ce9",
                        "entry_id": "d774618b-f904-3fa7-9300-6c66a8594e23",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The Grallina cyanoleuca that builds its nest with mud into a bowl-like shape."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Australia"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
